</p/><p><strong>Summary: </strong>
Preparing for a face to face game of Stonetop, and considering how to approach the game and inevitable note taking.
</p>
        <p><time datetime="2026-07-08" title="2026-07-08">Last night</time> Teddy and Tricia hosted Brian and I for our first session of
<cite>Stonetop</cite>.  Teddy had prepared an amazing taco salad spread, which we
enjoyed before getting down to co-creating the characters and community.</p>
<p>My task for <time datetime="2026-07-09" title="2026-07-09">today</time> is to synthesize my notes, everyone else’s characters, and
begin to develop the initial threats and starting adventure/situation.  I’m
opting to use a bound notebook for all of my notes.  My goal is to create a
durable artifact that I can keep on my shelf as a reminder of time well spent
with friends.</p>
<h1 id="brief-overview">Brief Overview</h1>
<p>Throughout the character session, we were all asking questions of each other’s
characters.  And offering world-building recommendations.</p>
<p>Interestingly, all of the characters were new to Stonetop.  Ezra (Seeker) from
Lygos.  Tomatu (Fox) originally from Stonetop but recently returned from decades
in Barrier Pass.  And Hiltrude (Heavy) from the northern-most Manmarch.</p>
<p>Ezra had arrived the summer prior.  Tomatu returned a couple of years ago.  And
Hiltrude arrived a month from the start date during a winter blizzard.  And in
the <em>Spring burst forth on the land</em> move, we got a <strong>6-</strong>; I’m wondering if the
village might have a bit of a “now prove your worth” attitude towards the new
mouths to feed?</p>
<h1 id="notetaking">Notetaking</h1>
<p>I’m now thinking about how I’ll be using the bound notebook for preparing and
running the game.  It makes sense that while running I have a spread (that is
two pages).  I envision a portion of one page of the spread as having questions
and textures to include in the session.  Leaving the majority of space for the
notes I take during play.</p>
<p>Working backwards, any draft work for the upcoming session would be on pages
after the previous session’s notes.  The notebook I chose for session 0 did not
have a dedicated index page and I opted to start my notes on the first page.
Which leaves me feeling a bit uneasy about the notebook I chose.</p>
<h1 id="were-i-to-use-my-computer-for-notes">Were I to Use My Computer for Notes</h1>
<p>I’m inclined to not use my computer for the note-taking; in part because I’m not
going to be using it during game-play.  Were I to use my computer for
note-taking, I’d want to “print out” the session preparation page; leaving space
on those pages notes.</p>
<p>Further, those preparatory notes would be a subset of my total set of notes.
All of which begins to sound like premature optimization and turning fun into
toil.  So I’m holding off and instead “playing” with pencil and paper.  In part
because paper and pencil have greater liberties.</p>
<p>Also, I spend enough time on a computer.  And feel like I should explore another
space.  Doodle on my notes.  Let the page be more of a canvas.</p>
<h1 id="rituals">Rituals</h1>
<p>I’m also reading <cite data-id="1A1FF400-CAC2-4955-ADC4-FF346E3E4D20">The Disappearance of Rituals</cite> by Byung-Chul Han.  As I write I’m thinking about Yes Indie’d Pod’s interview “10
Years of Making Stonetop” with Jeremy Strandberg—creator of
<cite>Stonetop</cite>.</p>
<p>Jeremy Strandberg highlighted that the homecoming procedure—dare I say
ritual—was crucial for creating connections between the player characters and
their community.  In games where they skipped that procedure, the characters
seemed to have a weaker attachment to the village.</p>
<p>I carried both this interview and my reading into our first session.  I used
<cite>Stonetop</cite>almost as a liturgical text, guiding us through our
character creation.</p>
<h1 id="conclusion">Conclusion</h1>
<p>This is my first face to face game since running <cite><a href="https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/400164/Errant?affiliate_id=318171">Errant</a></cite> <small><a class="ref" rel="tag opener" aria-label="Other site-wide references of “Errant”" title="Other site-wide references of “Errant”" href="https://takeonrules.com/site-map/glossary/#abbr-dfn-GLOSSARY-ERRANT">&#128214;</a></small>
 in <time datetime="2023" title="2023">2023</time>.  It felt good
to return to a <span><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Powered_by_the_Apocalypse">Powered by the Apocalypse</a></span> (<abbr title="Powered by the Apocalypse">PbtA</abbr> <small><a class="ref" rel="tag opener" aria-label="Other site-wide references of “Powered by the Apocalypse”" title="Other site-wide references of “Powered by the Apocalypse”" href="https://takeonrules.com/site-map/glossary/#abbr-dfn-GLOSSARY-PBTA">&#128214;</a></small>)
 game that is questions forward; by which I mean these
questions draw the players into the world, by giving them authority, but also in
their framing declaring what is somewhat established.  The questions provide the
lines, the answers provide the color.</p>
<p>Other games can and do do this, but in the case of <cite>Stonetope</cite>, the
game continues to be questions forward.  Not just with the ubiquitous “What do
you do?” but all throughout its setting book.  Thus making it easier to “play to
find out.”</p>
<p>I’m excited to get about the work of synthesizing, but used this post as a means
to think a bit about how I’m going to approach note taking.  Further, as I was
writing, I found my background thoughts beginning to draw connections and wonder
more about the nutrient rich material we worked through <time datetime="2026-07-08" title="2026-07-08">yesterday</time>.</p>

